                                                   INSTALL(LOCAL)NAME     install - procedures for installing UNaXcessSYNOPSIS     sh install.shDESCRIPTION     The UNaXcess conferencing system is largely self-installing     via the shell script _ˆi_ˆn_ˆs_ˆt_ˆa_ˆl_ˆl._ˆs_ˆh.  However, it helps to     understand what the UNaXcess install script is doing, and     what might be done beforehand.     UNaXcess prefers to be installed setuid to a particular     user, usually the login of the sysop.  This permits UNaXcess     to configure itself at runtime, requiring only that the file     _ˆu_ˆa-_ˆc_ˆo_ˆn_ˆf_ˆi_ˆg exist in the home directory of the user to which     it is setuid.  It also provides better security, as the     UNaXcess directory can be locked up tight, whereas write     permission must exist in ALL directories and files used by     UNaXcess if it is not setuid.COMPILING UNaXcess     At compile time, only two parameters need be set.  The sys-     tem type must be specified in the Makefile; a list of valid     system types is provided there.  This is required because     there are a number of divergent versions of Unix(R) in the     marketplace:  System V, Berkeley 4.2/4.3, Research Edition     7, Xenix, etc.  It is the author's hope that this will     change someday.     The second parameter is the NOAUTOPATH directive.  This     informs UNaXcess that it willnot be run setuid; it has a     hard-coded initial directory specified by the value of NOAU-     TOPATH, where it must find the _ˆu_ˆa-_ˆc_ˆo_ˆn_ˆf_ˆi_ˆg file and, if the     bbs-directory parameter is not listed within that file (see     _ˆC_ˆO_ˆN_ˆF_ˆI_ˆG_ˆU_ˆR_ˆA_ˆT_ˆI_ˆO_ˆN _ˆP_ˆA_ˆR_ˆA_ˆM_ˆE_ˆT_ˆE_ˆR_ˆS below), all other UNaXcess files     must also reside within that directory or below it.  This     directive should be specified as:                  -DNOAUTOPATH='"/path/to/UNaXcess"'     The best way to specify the system type parameter, on sys-     tems which support it, is to specify it in the _ˆm_ˆa_ˆk_ˆe command     used to make UNaXcess, such as ``make SYSTEM=SYS5 all''.  It     can also be placed on the CFLAGS line in the Makefile.INSTALLATION     UNaXcess is installed by running the command, ``sh     install.sh'', from the UNaXcess source directory.  It first     checks to see if NOAUTOPATH has been defined; if so, it dis-     ables all permission tightening and prevents the installa-     tion of the special _ˆm_ˆk_ˆc_ˆo_ˆn_ˆf command used by the setuid                                                                1INSTALL(LOCAL)     UNaXcess program.  If NOAUTOPATH is not defined, it asks for     a user name to which UNaXcess should be setuid; if the user     already exists, the installer must be logged in as the UNaX-     cess sysop, otherwise the script must be run as root.  (This     is unavoidable, as there is no other way to make a user.)     Note, however, that the _ˆm_ˆk_ˆc_ˆo_ˆn_ˆf command MUST be setuid root     to work.  Again, this is unavoidable; UNIX(R) does not allow     non-root users to create directories, and it is not possible     to make the setuid the real uid, so _ˆm_ˆk_ˆd_ˆi_ˆr would run as the     logged-in user and should not have permission to create a     directory using _ˆm_ˆk_ˆd_ˆi_ˆr.     The install script asks a number of questions.  The first     questions deal with the installation of the executable     files, and whether the optional programs should be     installed.  (An optional program is one like _ˆu_ˆa_ˆn_ˆe_ˆw_ˆs, which     will probably not be used on most systems.)  A second group     of questions is used to build the initial _ˆu_ˆa-_ˆc_ˆo_ˆn_ˆf_ˆi_ˆg file.     Finally, the install script invokes the _ˆu_ˆm_ˆo_ˆt_ˆd command to     create an initial bulletin.     The install script does not cover the creation of special     login messages, the new-user message, or other special-     purpose files; these are described and instructions for     creating them are given in the UNaXcess Administrator's     Manual.CONFIGURATION PARAMETERS     UNaXcess is configured at runtime via the file ua-config in     the home directory of UNaXcess's owner.  (Or the directory     specified by NOAUTOPATH.)  This file contains variable set-     tings of the form                              _ˆn_ˆa_ˆm_ˆe _ˆv_ˆa_ˆl_ˆu_ˆe     which affect UNaXcess's operation.  They are of three types:     STRING, which may take any whitespace-delimited string or     any string in quotes; NUMERIC, which may take any number     from 0-255; and BOOLEAN, which may take the strings YES, NO,     TRUE, or FALSE.  The variables are:          bbs-directory (STRING) - The directory where               UNaXcess's data files and message base               reside.  The default is UNaXcess's owner's               home directory.          readonly (BOOLEAN) - If this is TRUE, UNaXcess               will permit the creation of read-only confer-               ences.  (See the UNaXcess Administrator's               Manual for details.)  The default is FALSE.2                                                   INSTALL(LOCAL)          restricted (BOOLEAN) - If this is TRUE, UNaXcess               will permit restrictions on conference               membership via the M command.  See the UNaX-               cess Administrator's Manual for details.  The               default is FALSE.          shell (STRING) - This is the path of the default               shell used by the C command.  See also the               read-env variable.  The default is /bin/sh.          read-env (BOOLEAN) - If this is TRUE, UNaXcess               will read the environment of the invoker to               determine the shell to be run via the C com-               mand.  The default is FALSE.          bbs-user (STRING) - A list of UNIX(R) user-names,               separated by commas and/or spaces, which may               not log directly into UNaXcess.  This is               intended to prevent a generic BBS login from               conflicting with a UNIX(R) user; otherwise,               there would be a security hole, as shell               users are forced to log in by their UNIX(R)               user name if they are so named in UNaXcess.               This is explained in more detail (and more               clearly) in the UNaXcess  Administrator's               Manual.  The default is no BBS logins.          time-limit (NUMERIC) - The time limit, in minutes,               of a UNaXcess session.  If this is set to 0,               no time limit is imposed.  The default is 30               minutes.          sysop (STRING) - This variable defines the sysop's               login name.  The default is SYSOP; however,               special purpose BBSes may have this changed               to be more relevant (as in DUNGEON MASTER for               a fantasy-gaming BBS).          private-msgs (BOOLEAN) - If this is TRUE, users               are permitted to create private messages to               other users.  While public messages may have               any recipient named, private messages must be               to a registered UNaXcess user.          logging (BOOLEAN) - If this is TRUE, a log of               activity and errors will be kept in the file               Logfile in the directory defined by bbs-               directory.  This log can get quite large very               quickly.  The default is FALSE.          banner (STRING) - This is the pathname (fully               qualified or relative to bbs-directory) of a                                                                3INSTALL(LOCAL)               file to be displayed before the UNaXcess               login prompt is displayed.  The default               value, "", causes an internal message to be               displayed consisting of a copyright message.          login-msg (STRING) - This is the login prompt to               be displayed at the start of a UNaXcess ses-               sion.  The default value, "", causes the mes-               sage                   Enter your name, GUEST, OFF, or NEW:               to be displayed.          login-tries (NUMERIC) - This variable defines the               number of tries a user gets to log in to               UNaXcess.  A value of 0 will be treated as 1.               The default is 3.          ascii-upload (STRING) - The command to be passed               to /bin/sh in order to upload a file using               ASCII.  The default uses /bin/cat after turn-               ing echo off, then restores echo.  All down-               load commands may have the escape %s in them               to indicate where the file name should be               placed; if it is not present, UNaXcess will               append the file name to the end of the com-               mand, preceded by a space.          ascii-download (STRING) - The command to be passed               to /bin/sh in order to download a file using               ASCII.  The default uses /bin/cat to send the               file.          xmodem-upload (STRING) - The command passed to               /bin/sh to upload a file using XMODEM proto-               col.  The default is umodem -rb.          xmodem-upload (STRING) - The command passed to               /bin/sh to download a file using XMODEM pro-               tocol.  The default is umodem -sb.          kermit-upload (STRING) - The command passed to               /bin/sh to upload a file using Kermit proto-               col.  The default is kermit -iwr, and assumes               that C-Kermit is available.          kermit-download (STRING) - The command passed to               /bin/sh to download a file using Kermit pro-               tocol.  The default is kermit -iws, and               assumes that C-Kermit is available.4                                                   INSTALL(LOCAL)OTHER INSTALLABLE FILES     There are a number of other files used by UNaXcess that     should be updated at install time.  They all reside in the     bbs-directory:          NewMessage - This file contains the message shown               to new users.  As distributed, it contains a               copyright message.  It should detail the               system's rules, information about access lev-               els, and any other information for new users.          directory - This file contains listings of UDL               branches and downloadable files.  As distri-               buted, it defines the "general" UDL branch               and the UNaXcess User's Guide within that               branch.  See the UNaXcess Administrator's               Manual for information on maintaining this               file.ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS AND COPYRIGHTS     UNIX(R) is a registered trademark of AT&T.COPYRIGHT     UNaXcess is Copyright (C) 1983-1987 by Brandon S. Allbery.     Permission is granted to copy this program and to distribute     it freely; however, the author reserves the right to distri-     bute the program for any fees beyond a copying/handling fee.                                                                5␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀␀          